WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.340
Eric Edson:&nbsp; If you write badly, for instance…I’m
trying to think of a good example.&nbsp;

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:10.920
How about when I wrote my book, when I had
trimmed it down, nice and tight and I thought

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.360
I really had it ready to go.&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:18.220
I had a 120,000 or 130,000 words in the manuscript.&nbsp;

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.480
And I called them up and said “Okay, I’m
ready to go.&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.189
I’ve done my cutting and…here it is!&nbsp;

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:29.550
130,000 words and he laughed (the publisher
laughed) and said “No Eric.&nbsp;

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:37.090
We don’t publish anything over 70,000 words”
and it was like this bolt of horror that hit

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.090
me.&nbsp;

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.170
And I said “Wait a minute!

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:44.020
Are you actually telling me that I have to
cut my book in half?”&nbsp;

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.370
And he said “Yeah…actually…I’m afraid
I am.”

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:57.370
So for 3 or 4 days I was just practically
in a coma but…any writer…you’re appalled

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.180
by the way and what they make you do to your
babies.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.320
&nbsp; And then I got some help.&nbsp;

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.460
I had somebody else, a dear, dear friend who
was good with editing take a look at it.&nbsp;

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.590
And I seriously cut into it one more time
for editing.&nbsp;

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.100
I got it down to 82,000 words.&nbsp;

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.240
Not quite their 70,000.&nbsp;

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:26.930
But I cut out by 40-45% of the book that I
thought was absolutely necessary, it was gone

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.341
and the book was better for it.&nbsp;

00:01:28.341 --> 00:01:30.909
It was such a better book.&nbsp;

00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:33.200
It was clearer and it was cleaner.&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.150
And there were so many swamps removed from
it.&nbsp;

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:41.899
It was an interesting experience.&nbsp;

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:48.899
But I did not realize then…you have to re-objectify
and I didn’t realize yet how much work there

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:51.200
was yet to be done.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:02:00.330
So writing is word choices and a process of
re-objectification that is something critical,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.330
too.&nbsp;

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:07.030
Which means writers never write alone.&nbsp;

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.840
Never.&nbsp;

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.180
But we spend a lot of time alone in the writing
process.&nbsp;

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.819
Once we have something, then we have to (and
this is important, and this is important to

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:25.840
new writers everywhere), you have to have
a circle of new people whose opinion of material

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.840
you trust.&nbsp;

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.780
It’s one of the things that happens in our
program.&nbsp;

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.599
These people make friends for life because
then they rely on each other’s judgment

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.750
because everybody knows everybody’s opinion
grows to become quite sophisticated about

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.040
the material and you can always call up one
of your buddies and say “You want to do

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.450
a script swap?”

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.510
You read mine.&nbsp;

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.819
I read yours.&nbsp;

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:50.860
And what you get back (frequently) if not
what you want to hear.&nbsp;

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.060
We’re all children.&nbsp;

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.471
We all want to say “Oh!&nbsp;

00:02:53.471 --> 00:02:55.750
That is the greatest thing.&nbsp;

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:56.750
Isn’t that great!”&nbsp;

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.129
And that is never what you hear so you’re
always eternally disappointed.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:07.730
So you hear objective, good criticism of what
you’ve got and I always tell them 48 hours.&nbsp;

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.709
Don’t do anything for 48 hours.&nbsp;

00:03:09.709 --> 00:03:10.790
Suffer.&nbsp;

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.379
Fine.&nbsp;

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:13.379
Stay in bed.&nbsp;

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:20.209
Whatever you need in response to what you’ve
heard about your material and then sit down

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:23.670
and read it again (cover-to-cover).

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:31.709
And it will be revealed to you through someone
else’s point of view what you really have.&nbsp;

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:36.040
And all of the sudden it’s time to start
editing again and rewriting again you will

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.040
get it.&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:43.980
And what has happened there is that the cycle
of subjective work to re-objectification.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.700
Because the more you work on something over
time, the more you are in it.&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:53.450
You are drawn into it and it becomes within
you and it is about you and you think you’re

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:58.900
doing something great and then you take it
out and you get a slap in the face or two,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.159
it clears your eyes, it clears your vision
very, very well.&nbsp;

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:09.819
You become re-objectified and you approach
the material again with clean eyes for another

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:11.030
go-around.&nbsp;

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:18.540
And in my experience (if you really want good
material) and I’m assuming we all do, there

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.980
are some who just want pats on the head and
all that kind of stuff.&nbsp;

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:27.060
But if you really want the material to be
good, you have to go through that cycle, subjectivity/re-objectification,&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:34.420
subjectivity/re-objectification, four or five,
maybe six times.&nbsp;

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:41.750
Then…then…each time, it gets better…and
it gets a little better…and a little better.&nbsp;

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.420
And you don’t want to send anything out.&nbsp;

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.850
It’s one of the biggest mistakes people
make, sending out material before it’s finished.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.500
&nbsp; Film Courage:&nbsp; So if you’re giving writers
the permission to write badly and you’re

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.820
doing these exercises and that is one of the
first things you write on the board (when

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:01.220
you start class), what permission are you
giving them with re-writes?&nbsp;

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.040
And how open are they to re-writing?

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.470
Eric Edson:&nbsp; Most of them get it.&nbsp;

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:15.000
I mean it’s a cliché, but clichés are
clichés because they’re true that writing

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.890
is re-writing.&nbsp;

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:21.050
And the only real way to teach writing is
through editing.&nbsp;

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:25.110
That’s why I spend a great deal of time
on scripts and sample editing and scratching

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.020
out stuff and throwing out scenes.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.680
And telling people “You don’t need this”
and “This is good.&nbsp;

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:36.160
Expand it here” and all that kind of stuff
because it’s stuff that people truly need

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.160
to hear.&nbsp;

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.070
That is what you get in a concentrated two-year,
four semester program.&nbsp;

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:48.570
You get a heck of a lot of practice in a very
short period of time, surrounded by very knowledgeable

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:51.020
people who can guide you.&nbsp;

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.300
Is there wear and tear on the nervous system?

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.320
You bet.&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.590
That’s kind of built in and guaranteed.&nbsp;

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:02.490
But so far, I’ve never heard anyone say
they regret it or anybody complain about it

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:05.720
when they come out the other end.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:13.740
The other thing I write on the board a few
weeks later is “There is no such thing as

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:20.050
a bad screenplay, only an unfinished one.”&nbsp;

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:25.070
And this is something I truly believe just
as strongly as I believe about you’ve got

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.090
to write badly with pride or you’re never
going to write anything.&nbsp;

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:33.870
You have to allow yourself to do that.&nbsp;

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:43.540
But I think any idea for a screenplay that
is conflict-based (this is the essential),

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.600
it has to be conflict based.&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:53.370
But any idea for a screenplay that is conflict-based
can be made to work.&nbsp;

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:54.990
It can.&nbsp;

00:06:54.990 --> 00:07:01.710
And bad screenplays (if they contain some
conflict), they can all be made to work, it’s

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:04.650
just that they’re sent out before they’re
finished.&nbsp;

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:09.930
And when you send something out before it’s
really finished, you really only get one good

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:13.180
shot at it and then you kind of burn it up.&nbsp;

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:20.630
And a lot of good ideas have gone to the idea
cemetery because of making that move.

